The mechanism. Persuasion at scale rarely argues with your values. It
wears them. The pitch arrives dressed as the thing you already believe in,
which is why it slips past your defenses. It doesnʼt control you. It just gets
a lot of free rides on your identity.
The rule. Bernays had newspapers and a parade. The systems aimed at
you have your location, your history, and a mountain of data about what
makes people click. Before you act on a want, ask: who profits if I feel
this? Then hold me to the same test: food first should earn its force from a
meal somebody can actually get.
The practice.
Run a desire audit for one week. Every time you notice a strong,
sudden want, a purchase, an outrage, an urge to check something,
write one line: what I want, when it arrived, and who profits if I act on
it. You donʼt have to resist anything. Just log it. By day four youʼll start
seeing the parade routes.
Trace one recent opinion back to its torch moment. Pick something
you feel strongly about now that you didnʼt a year ago, and reconstruct
how it got in: which feed, which framing, which part of your identity it
flattered.
Do the firewall work as physical acts, today: notifications off, phone in
a drawer that closes, one real thing before noon.
